; Minimal probe for stringSubstring (contract entry 38): one node.
;
; Every call here passes `start = 0`, and that is a hard constraint rather than
; an oversight. The written arguments are `(start, end)`: the registry declares
; `paramNames: ["start", "end"]` and the contract records the same. The LLVM
; emitter still reads the second argument as a **length** (a filed B-group
; deviation, the emitter side being the deviating one). The two readings
; produce the same character range exactly when `start == 0`, so this fixture
; can pin the buffer, the copy and the terminator, but it cannot pin the
; distinction itself -- a call with a non-zero start would fail the harness on
; purpose. Pinning that needs a test asserting the channels differ, not a
; fixture asserting they agree.
;
; What is covered: an interior slice, the empty result, the whole string, and a
; bound at the receiver's exact length. Bounds beyond it are left out on
; purpose -- under the length reading they read past the receiver, which is a
; property of the deviation, not of this node.

main|func() -> ():
    var s = "Hello, World"
    print(s.substring(0, 5))
    print(s.substring(0, 0))
    print(s.substring(0, 12))
    print(s)
    print("abcdef".substring(0, 1))
    print("abcdef".substring(0, 6))
    return
